    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d461">

                <pb facs="0548" n="436" sameAs="frus:frus1919Russia/pg436"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.01/163a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Commission to
                        Negotiate Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-01T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 1,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>3296. For Polk.</p>
                <p>You are confidentially informed that as soon as President Wilson is able to
                    attend to business the Secretary is seriously considering recommending to him
                    that formal recognition be granted to the Omsk Government. It is, however, the
                    wish of the Secretary that you submit whatever information you can give as to
                    the attitude <pb facs="0549" n="437" xml:id="pg437"/> on this subject of the
                    other members of the Council. The Department naturally wishes that the United
                    States should take the lead if Kolchak is to be recognized, but I desire your
                    opinion as to whether a simultaneous recognition by the five Powers which
                    collectively agreed last May and June to aid the Omsk Government with food,
                    supplies, and munitions would not be better.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
